💡 Approach

We use:

1. GROUP BY product_id

We group all sales belonging to the same product.

2. MIN(sale_date)

Finds the first sale date of each product.

MIN(sale_date)

We make sure the first sale is not before January 1, 2019:

MIN(sale_date) >= '2019-01-01'

3. MAX(sale_date)

Finds the last sale date of each product.

MAX(sale_date)

We make sure the last sale is not after March 31, 2019:

MAX(sale_date) <= '2019-03-31'

4. HAVING

Because MIN() and MAX() are aggregate functions, we use HAVING instead of WHERE.

✅ MySQL Solution

SELECT product_id, product_name
FROM Product
WHERE product_id IN (
    SELECT product_id
    FROM Sales
    GROUP BY product_id
    HAVING MIN(sale_date) >= '2019-01-01'
       AND MAX(sale_date) <= '2019-03-31'
);

🔍 How the Query Works

The subquery:

SELECT product_id
FROM Sales
GROUP BY product_id
HAVING MIN(sale_date) >= '2019-01-01'
   AND MAX(sale_date) <= '2019-03-31'

first identifies products whose entire sales history falls within Q1 2019.

