⭐ Important Interview Concept

A common mistake is to write:

WHERE sale_date BETWEEN '2019-01-01' AND '2019-03-31'

This is not sufficient.

Why?

Because this only checks individual sales within the date range. A product could have:

2019-01-10
2019-02-15
2019-03-20
2019-04-10

If we simply filter the sales between January and March, the April sale would be ignored.

Using:

MIN(sale_date)

and

MAX(sale_date)

allows us to check the complete sales history of each product.

🚀 Alternative Solution Using JOIN

The same problem can also be solved using a JOIN:

SELECT p.product_id, p.product_name
FROM Product p
JOIN Sales s
    ON p.product_id = s.product_id
GROUP BY p.product_id, p.product_name
HAVING MIN(s.sale_date) >= '2019-01-01'
   AND MAX(s.sale_date) <= '2019-03-31';

This avoids the IN subquery and directly groups the joined data.

📊 Complexity

Assuming Sales contains N rows:

Time Complexity: approximately O(N)

Space Complexity: depends on the number of distinct products being grouped.

With an appropriate index on Sales(product_id, sale_date), the query can perform efficiently on large datasets.

🎯 Takeaway

The main idea is:

To find entities whose entire activity falls within a date range, use MIN() and MAX() with GROUP BY and HAVING.

This is a very common SQL interview pattern for Data Analyst, Data Engineer, and BI roles.
